Phase 3 Spectral Analysis — Test v2 (with ACCEPT comparison)
Date: 2026-05-15
Test clusters: Abell_0068, Abell_0209, Abell_0611, MACSJ0429

=== Background ===
Previous test (Abell_0068 only) showed T_X=33.4 keV vs ACCEPT T_X=7.99 keV.
This session tested 4 clusters with both full R500 and core-excised (0.15R500-R500) modes.
ACCEPT reference data created at configs/accept_reference.csv.

=== Results ===

| Cluster     | z     | R500(") | Full R500 T_X | CE T_X  | ACCEPT T_X | Full rstat | Status |
|-------------|-------|---------|---------------|---------|------------|------------|--------|
| Abell_0068  | 0.255 | 310     | 33.4 keV      | 53.9    | 7.99       | 1.15       | Bad    |
| Abell_0209  | 0.206 | 451     | 21.0 keV      | 40.0    | 8.28       | 2.14       | Bad    |
| Abell_0611  | 0.288 | 309     | 63.9 keV      | 64.0    | 6.69       | 2.57       | Bad    |
| MACSJ0429   | 0.399 | 261     | 7.56 keV      | 12.9    | 6.76       | 0.95       | GOOD   |

=== Key Findings ===

1. MACSJ0429 (z=0.40, R500=261") fits well: T_X=7.56 keV, only +12% from ACCEPT=6.76 keV.
   This proves the pipeline code is fundamentally correct.

2. Core excision does NOT solve the problem — makes it worse for most clusters.
   The high T_X is NOT caused by cool-core contamination.

3. The problem is systematic: low-z clusters (large R500 on sky) have local annulus
   background contaminated by ICM emission. The background region (1.2-1.8 R500)
   still contains significant cluster emission for nearby clusters.

4. Abell_0209 with chi2gehrels+subtract+group(25) gives T_X=16.1 keV (rstat=0.94),
   better than WSTAT but still 2x ACCEPT. Confirms background subtraction issue.

=== Root Cause ===
Local annulus background is inappropriate for clusters where R500 > ~300 arcsec
because the ICM surface brightness is still significant at 1.2-1.8 R500.
This is a well-known limitation documented in CLAUDE.md.

Literature standard: blank-sky background with 10-12 keV normalization
(Vikhlinin+2006, Donahue+2014).

=== Next Steps ===
Options to proceed:
A) Implement blank-sky background handling (complex, but literature standard)
B) Use background region further out (>2 R500) where available
C) Accept the systematic and use model-dependent corrections
D) Focus only on high-z clusters where current method works (z>0.35)
